{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310826,"nonce":"WXxOa3WsAFCD0EU6ElkgZu6bfAE=","title":"Four follow-up tasks identified by audit during the inliner SSA-counter fix (commit 655d7ac). Not blocking but worth landing to prevent ...","message":"Audit during commit 655d7ac (`inlining: sync caller.next_reg_id after inline_call_site`) surfaced four hardening opportunities. None blocked the fix but all are worth landing to make the W0020 bug class structurally unreachable rather than relying on per-pass discipline.\n\n\n\nAdd a `SsaValidatorPass` that runs immediately after the optimization pipeline in `compiler/src/ir/optimization.rs::PassManager::run`. Assertions:\n\n- **No duplicate dest IrIds per function**: scan every block's phi nodes + instructions, error if any IrId appears as `dest` more than once.\n- **next_reg_id \u003e max(IrId)**: assert `function.next_reg_id \u003e max(dest IrIds)`. Catches counter desync the moment it happens.\n- **Phi incoming completeness**: `phi.incoming.len() == predecessors(block).len()` for the defining block.\n\nGate behind `cfg!(debug_assertions)` so production builds stay fast. In debug/test it catches the same bug class at the IR level rather than at Cranelift codegen (where it manifests as W0020 trap-stub install, much harder to diagnose).\n\nImplementation effort: ~150 LOC. Run order: last pass in `PassManager`. Would have caught the inliner bug immediately without 4 sessions of bisection.\n\n\n`compiler/src/ir/hir_to_mir.rs` lines 3544-3614 implement a `SavedLoweringState` for nested function switching (async closures, lambdas). The audit noted that this save/restore does NOT include MIR builder register-allocation state. Verify:\n\n- No parent-context allocations bracket the save point.\n- Pass 1 → Pass 2 transitions preserve monotonic `next_reg_id`.\n- Lambda body lowering doesn't reset the counter mid-function.\n\nAdd a debug assertion at the save/restore boundary to catch future regressions.\n\n\n`compiler/src/ir/hir_to_mir.rs:31801-31829` manually assigns IrIds to lambda parameters. `IrFunction::new()` (functions.rs:266-268) then reallocates the same parameter regs at construction, making the manual writes dead. Two options:\n\n- Remove the manual assignment (verify nothing depends on the pre-new IrIds).\n- Document why they exist with a comment + assertion.\n\nRisk: low. Effort: half day. Worth doing for codebase clarity.\n\n\nThe compiler currently has THREE distinct register-allocation models that don't share interfaces:\n\n- **MirBuilder** (`mir_builder.rs:62`): per-builder `next_reg_id`, used by hir_to_mir.\n- **IrBuilder** (`builder.rs`): no own field, delegates to per-function.\n- **Optimization passes**: track a local counter, must sync back to `function.next_reg_id`. The model that just had the bug.\n\nAdd a 30-line module-level doc comment at the top of `compiler/src/ir/mod.rs` documenting all three, when each applies, and the invariant each must preserve. Prevents future refactors from reintroducing the InliningPass-shaped bug.\n\n\n- **#1** unblocks better debugging for any future MIR-level bugs. Land within the next week or two if any new optimization pass is being written.\n- **#2** is a real audit, not a sweep — only worth it if we add async/closure-heavy code paths.\n- **#3** is hygiene; do it when touching that file for another reason.\n- **#4** is documentation; pair with the next pass refactor.\n\n\n- [[bugs-w0020-value-map-miss-cross-loop]] — the bug this audit surfaced from. RESOLVED at 655d7ac.\n- [[bugs-trap-stub-cascade]] — the silent-fail mechanism downstream of W0020 that made this bug class hard to diagnose. #1 (SSA validator) would catch the source instead of waiting for codegen.\n- [[feedback-no-silent-dispatch-fallthrough]] — same principle: when invariants break, ERROR early; never silently fall through. #1 is that principle for the optimization pipeline.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_compiler_ssa_hygiene_followups` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}